meta {
  name: 06 - Export Structured JSON (extracted table)
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/ocr/export/{{docId}}?format=json
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-API-Key: {{apiKey}}
}

params:query {
  format: json
}

docs {
  The extracted table as a downloadable export file.

  Requests 02 and 05 now carry structured_data directly, so this is no longer
  the only way to reach the table - reach for it when you want the export file
  itself, or one of the other formats below.

  ## Response

  filename, document_type, project_name, markdown, and structured_data.

  For a stock statement, structured_data.fields carries distributor_name,
  company_name, statement_period, summary_totals, columns, rows, and items -
  where each item holds item_code, product_name, pack, batch, expiry, rate,
  opening_qty, receipt_qty, issue_qty, closing_qty, calculated_closing_qty,
  discrepancy and validation_status.

  Items carry no raw_cells: it was a byte-for-byte copy of the matching entry
  in `rows`, so every cell crossed the wire twice. Read `rows`, which is
  positionally aligned with `columns`.

  ## format values

  - json  application/json, the shape above (markdown + structured JSON)
  - txt   text/plain, structured JSON block then plain text (server default)
  - md    same as txt
  - csv   text/csv, one summary row plus the structured fields

  ## Note for integrators

  The response is sent as a file download - it carries a
  Content-Disposition: attachment header - so some HTTP clients will save it
  rather than hand you the body. The bytes are ordinary JSON either way.

  ## Errors

  - 404 document not found, or results missing for that document
}
